00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:42,080
 around the material you're dealing with and they're normally documented at atmospheric pressure.

46
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,760
 Over the two, flash point is usually the metric that we use to categorize materials

47
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:50,400
 as either combustible, meaning that they can burn and flammable, meaning that they're easily

48
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:52,560
 ignitable at ambient conditions.

49
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:56,160
 There's a third important temperature that you need to consider when you're thinking about

50
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:59,680
 flammability of materials, the auto-ignition temperature.

51
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:03,680
 As the name suggests, this is the lowest temperature at which the vapors of a material will

52
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:08,560
 not only ignite in air when given a source of flame and not only ignite in air when given a source

