00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:16,720
 Like the others, auto-ignition temperature is normally documented at standard conditions,

55
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:21,600
 in other words, pressure-wide atmosphere and oxygen at atmospheric concentration.

56
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:26,240
 But at higher oxygen concentrations and at higher pressures, this temperature decreases

57
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:28,560
 in things auto-ignite more easily.

58
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:33,040
 You need to be careful about this if you're designing components to live in a highly oxygenated

59
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:36,880
 environment or if you're designing components to live in a highly pressurized environment.

60
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,200
 There are a range of tests that we use to characterize the flammability of engineering materials.

61
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,440
 They all generally involve trying to light your material with a bunch of burner or a cigarette or

62
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:50,320
 something and recording whether that material ignited at all and if so how easy it was to do that,

63
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:54,560
 whether and how quickly the flame is spread, whether the material had any self-extinguishing

64
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:58,400
 capabilities, the rate at which heat was released from the burning material, whether

65
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,400
 or not the material melted or charred, potentially shedding flaming parts of itself,

66
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,440
 whether you're burning material let off any toxic smoke or gas,

67
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:09,680
 and whether any of these properties change burning in material vertically versus horizontally.

68
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:15,280
 For example, UL94 is a standard test for checking the flammability of engineering plastics.

69
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:19,120
 In this test you expose a sample of your material to a bunch of burner and you check to see

70
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:25,200
 if it ignites, whether it's self-extinguishes and whether as it burns it drips any flaming material.

71
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:30,560
 It makes sense in the same context talking about flammability to mention intrinsically safe materials.

72
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,000
 There are many dangerous environments that you might find yourself working or supporting

73
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,280
 as an engineer where the ambient atmosphere has a higher concentration of flammable gases.

74
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:41,680
 A very common example of this is underground mining.

75
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:45,440
 I did a little bit of time working as a Christmas beetle and underground mine for my placement in

76
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:49,920
 undergrad. In these environments the higher concentrations of flammable gases in the atmosphere

